  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Reading them back from Kibana, the dashboard has all its panels and the rule's status is `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` data view has 14,074 web requests timestamped from Oct 4 to Dec 3, 2026, using the `timestamp` field. Each request records:\n- client IP\n- URL and request\n- response code\n- bytes\n- geo source and destination\n- OS, agent, extension and tags\n\nThe response code is stored as text, and only three codes appear: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-443292cf Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-443292cf-web-logs-overview-f59faf84b4cf\n- Single-number panels for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served.\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of response codes.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and bytes.\n- A bar chart of requests by source country (top 10).\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-443292cf 5xx spike\"** (id `753ccaa3-3cc6-4a54-b835-2b835a6667bb`):\n- It runs every minute against `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\", using the `timestamp` field.\n- It fires when there are more than 10 in the last 5 minutes.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires the alert shows up in Kibana but no notification is sent. Add a connector (Slack, email, etc.) if you want to be notified.",
